Subtask 2.2: Monitor Jobs and Troubleshoot Connectivity
Review job history: Navigate to Jobs, confirm at least three completed jobs are listed with status Successful. Click into the Deploy Web Servers job and confirm the Host Events tab shows ok or changed for managed-node-1 and managed-node-2.

Query job statistics via the API:

curl -u admin:password -k http://localhost/api/v2/jobs/?page_size=10
Expected result: A JSON response listing recent jobs with "status": "successful" for each entry created in this task.

Connectivity Troubleshooting (use this sequence whenever a job fails with "UNREACHABLE" or timeout errors):
# 1. Check basic network reachability from the AWX container
docker exec awx_web ping -c 3 managed-node-1

# 2. Check DNS resolution from inside the container
docker exec awx_web nslookup managed-node-1

# 3. Verify the SSH private key was mounted with correct permissions
docker exec awx_web ls -la /var/lib/awx/.ssh/

# 4. Test SSH manually with verbose output to see the exact failure point
docker exec awx_web ssh -v -i /var/lib/awx/.ssh/id_rsa user@managed-node-1
Interpret the results as follows:

If step 1 fails: the managed node is unreachable at the network level. Check firewall rules on the managed node (firewall-cmd --list-all) and confirm both nodes are on the same network/security group.
If step 2 fails but step 1 succeeds: DNS resolution is broken inside the container. Add an explicit /etc/hosts entry or use IP addresses instead of hostnames in the inventory.
If step 3 shows permissions other than 600 or shows the file missing: the volume mount failed or the key was not copied correctly; recheck the volumes section of docker-compose.yml and restart the web and task containers.
If step 4 fails with "Permission denied": the public key was not copied to the managed node's authorized_keys, or the wrong username is being used; rerun ssh-copy-id for that node.
If step 4 succeeds but AWX jobs still fail: check that the credential in AWX references the same private key and username used in this manual test.
Run a direct connectivity check from inside the AWX container using Ansible itself:
docker exec -it awx_web ansible all -i /var/lib/awx/projects/lab-project/inventories/hosts -m ping
Deliverable and expected result: Every host returns "ping": "pong" with a green SUCCESS status. If any host fails, apply the four-step Connectivity Troubleshooting sequence above before re-running job templates.

Task 3: Implement Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)
Subtask 3.1: Create Organizations and Teams
Create a new organization:

Navigate to Organizations > Add
Name: Development Team, Description: Development environment organization
Click Save
Create teams:

Navigate to Teams > Add
Name: Web Developers, Organization: Development Team, Description: Web development team
Click Save
Create a second team: Name: System Administrators, Organization: Default, Description: System administration team
Click Save
Deliverable and expected result: The Organizations list shows both Default and Development Team. The Teams list shows Web Developers (under Development Team) and System Administrators (under Default).

Subtask 3.2: Create Users, Assign Team Membership, and Configure Permissions
Create users:

Navigate to Users > Add
Username: webdev1, Email: webdev1@lab.com, First Name: Web, Last Name: Developer, Password: DevPassword123!, User Type: Normal User
Click Save
Repeat to create a second user: Username: sysadmin1, Email: sysadmin1@lab.com, First Name: System, Last Name: Administrator, Password: AdminPassword123!, User Type: Normal User
Click Save
Assign users to teams:

Navigate to Teams > Web Developers > Users tab
Click Add, select webdev1, click Save
Navigate to Teams > System Administrators > Users tab
Click Add, select sysadmin1, click Save
Grant team-level permissions on resources:

Navigate to Teams > Web Developers > Roles tab
Click Add, select the Lab Ansible Project project, assign role Use, click Save
Click Add again, select the Lab Environment inventory, assign role Read, click Save
Navigate to Teams > System Administrators > Roles tab
Click Add, select the Deploy Web Servers job template, assign role Admin, click Save
Click Add again, select the Lab Environment inventory, assign role Admin, click Save
Verify RBAC enforcement:

Log out of the admin account
Log in as webdev1 / DevPassword123!
Navigate to Templates: confirm webdev1 can view the Lab Ansible Project project but cannot see an Admin gear icon to delete it, and cannot launch Deploy Web Servers (the job template should not appear, since only the project and inventory were granted, not the template itself)
Log out, log back in as admin
Deliverable and expected result: Logging in as webdev1 confirms restricted access consistent with the Use/Read roles granted (no delete capability on the project, no visibility into job templates that were not explicitly shared). Logging in as sysadmin1 and launching Deploy Web Servers succeeds because that user's team was granted the Admin role on that template. This confirms RBAC is correctly scoping access by team membership and role assignment rather than granting blanket admin rights.

Expected Outcomes
A fully running AWX instance with a synced project, populated inventory, machine credential, and at least two successful job template executions plus one successful workflow execution
A documented, tested connectivity troubleshooting procedure that resolves the most common AWX-to-managed-node failure modes (network, DNS, SSH key permissions, and authentication)
A working RBAC configuration with a custom organization, two teams, two users, and verified role-scoped access differing from the admin superuser view
Conclusion
In this lab, you deployed a complete Ansible Automation Platform environment from the ground up, standing up AWX in Docker containers, wiring it to real managed nodes over SSH, and registering an inventory, credential, and Git-backed project. You then built job templates and a multi-step workflow, executed them successfully, and used the AWX API and container-level diagnostic commands to monitor job health and systematically troubleshoot connectivity failures between the AWX web container and managed nodes. Finally, you implemented role-based access control by creating an organization, teams, and users, and verified that permissions were correctly scoped so that non-admin users only had the access explicitly granted to their team. With these skills, you are prepared to operate an Ansible Automation Platform deployment in a production-like setting, extend it with additional projects and credential types, and apply finer-grained RBAC policies as your automation environment grows.
